WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.050
This course is about agents and how they
break.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.825
An agent is a program that lets a language
model call your code.

3
00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:12.050
You extend one, you attack it, and you
prove your fix held.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.200
Before any of that, you have three days
and one list to learn.

5
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.300
Your first deadline is the Module 0 Gate,
and it is due on day 3.

6
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.100
It is not just a grade. It is how the
college records that you are here.

7
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.226
Census falls on day 8. Miss the Gate and

8
00:00:29.226 --> 00:00:32.350
you can be recorded as never having
attended.

9
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.425
Nobody can fix that afterward, so start on
day 1.

10
00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:39.720
Attempts are unlimited and your best score

11
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.875
is kept. You need 100 percent to open Week
2.

12
00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:48.700
The first part is a quiz. Every question
comes from the Rules of Engagement page.

13
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.062
The second part is a code. You run the
verify

14
00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:55.275
env script in your lab folder and it
prints one.

15
00:00:55.625 --> 00:01:00.200
Your code is different from your
classmate's, so no answer key can exist.

16
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.400
The third part is a short written answer.

17
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.800
Start on day 1, because setup is the part
that goes wrong.

18
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.704
Now the rule that matters most. Here is

19
00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:13.650
clause 1, said out loud so you can repeat
it.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.099
You may attack the HelpDesk Lite app in
your lab

21
00:01:17.099 --> 00:01:21.425
repository. It runs on your computer and
it was built to be broken.

22
00:01:21.775 --> 00:01:24.575
You may attack your own copy of the agent.

23
00:01:24.925 --> 00:01:29.100
And you may attack a language model
running on your own computer.

24
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.400
That is the whole list. Nothing on it
belongs to anyone else.

25
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.006
Security testing is legal work when
someone has

26
00:01:37.006 --> 00:01:40.400
said yes. The word for that yes is
authorization.

27
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.425
This page is the yes you have, and it
names three things.

28
00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:48.075
Clause 2 is shorter, and it has no
exceptions.

29
00:01:48.425 --> 00:01:53.900
Not a commercial AI service. Not ChatGPT,
Claude, Gemini or Groq.

30
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:59.550
Not a system the college owns. Not Canvas,
not ACES, not campus Wi-Fi.

31
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.025
Not your job's systems, and not a friend's
or a family member's.

32
00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:09.300
I only tried it once is not a defense.
Neither is it was already broken.

33
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.373
Breaking either clause means a zero on
that

34
00:02:12.373 --> 00:02:15.350
work and a referral. It can also break the
law.

35
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.275
Your key is yours alone, and it lives in
one place.

36
00:02:19.625 --> 00:02:22.126
The settings file is tracked by git. A key

37
00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:24.925
written there is published the moment you
push.

38
00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:28.189
So your key goes in a file named dot e n
v,

39
00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:31.375
and nowhere else. That file is never
committed.

40
00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:34.625
The lab code checks for you. It refuses to
start

41
00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:37.525
when it finds a credential in the settings
file.

42
00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:41.438
If a key ever leaks, turn it off at the
provider and tell

43
00:02:41.438 --> 00:02:45.500
your instructor. Nobody is in trouble for
reporting that quickly.

44
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.675
The rhythm does not change after this
module.

45
00:02:49.025 --> 00:02:54.050
Every week opens with an Overview. It says
what is due and how long it takes.

46
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.050
Then a Content page. Read it before you
touch the lab.

47
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.250
Then the graded work.

48
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.250
Then a Wrap-up.

49
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.825
Assigned work runs 5 to 9 hours a week,
reading included.

50
00:03:07.175 --> 00:03:10.575
If coding is new to you, plan for 10 to
20.

51
00:03:10.925 --> 00:03:14.100
That is normal, not a sign of falling
behind.

52
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.987
Set one rule now. Thirty minutes of no

53
00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:19.925
progress, and you post on the Q and A
board.

54
00:03:20.275 --> 00:03:25.550
Include the exact command and the exact
error text. Never paste your key.

55
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.700
One sentence carries this whole course.
Scope first, technique second.

56
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:36.125
Permission comes before any technique you
learn here. That order never changes.

57
00:03:36.475 --> 00:03:39.308
So today, read the Rules of Engagement,
set

58
00:03:39.308 --> 00:03:42.075
up the lab, and run the environment check.

59
00:03:42.425 --> 00:03:45.171
Then take the Gate. You have until day 3,

60
00:03:45.171 --> 00:03:48.050
and you can retake it as often as you
need.
